(3587713, 485394, 46336, '<td valign="top">Hi Kim, <br/>This is a good effort, especially considering how new you are to tools like curves. Time and practise will go a long way. Given that this was an Advanced Editting challenge I would recommend you spend tiem really drawign those eyes and lashes out - focuing on the detail. This tutorial is brilliant: <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/tutorial.php?TUTORIAL_ID=54" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">linky</a>. <br/>Otherwise I think thi shot drew out 12 comments during voting, which is pretty good going. The points made below are pretty useful and are worth paying attention to. I think the blow-outs at the top are the biggest distraction from all that is good in the shot.</td>', 'Hi Kim, This is a good effort, especially considering how new you are to tools like curves. Time and practise will go a long way. Given that this was an Advanced Editting challenge I would recommend you spend tiem really drawign those eyes and lashes out - focuing on the detail. This tutorial is brilliant: linky. Otherwise I think thi shot drew out 12 comments during voting, which is pretty good going. The points made below are pretty useful and are worth paying attention to. I think the blow-outs at the top are the biggest distraction from all that is good in the shot.', '2007-04-27 08:37:29.000000', NULL, 0),
(3591452, 485522, 60862, '<td valign="top">I''m floored at the score.  I voted this an 8.  The clarity of his eyes, the spot-on focus, and his expression are all wonderful.  Yes, if I lean waaaayyyy in, I can see of the artifacts.  But I don''t vote with my nose 1/2" from the screen. :)<br/> <br/>As for those artifacts, try copying the original into a new layer, sharpening that layer, and then creating a layer mask and painting black in the layer mask of the sharpened layer, to remove it and let the original show through.  That essentially lets you do spot sharpening that''s reversible.</td>', 'I''m floored at the score.  I voted this an 8.  The clarity of his eyes, the spot-on focus, and his expression are all wonderful.  Yes, if I lean waaaayyyy in, I can see of the artifacts.  But I don''t vote with my nose 1/2" from the screen. :) As for those artifacts, try copying the original into a new layer, sharpening that layer, and then creating a layer mask and painting black in the layer mask of the sharpened layer, to remove it and let the original show through.  That essentially lets you do spot sharpening that''s reversible.', '2007-04-29 00:40:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(3602249, 485629, 20662, '<td valign="top">OMG! Look at her! She''s gotten soooo grown up. How will you ever survive? she''s beautiful!</td>', 'OMG! Look at her! She''s gotten soooo grown up. How will you ever survive? she''s beautiful!', '2007-05-01 20:39:24.000000', NULL, 0),
